        "original_id": "de611eb8-f4f2-5716-98e5-c49514548061",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Works: Iphigénie en Tauride; Der Barbier von Bagdad\n- People: Christoph Willibald Gluck; Peter Cornelius; Nicolas-François Guillard\n- Concepts: opera; libretto; One Thousand and One Nights\n\nFacts:\n- In 1779, Christoph Willibald Gluck's Iphigénie en Tauride premiered as an opera in four acts.\n- Nicolas-François Guillard's most famous work was the libretto for Iphigénie en Tauride, which was his first libretto and was set by Gluck after Gluck had initially rejected it.\n- Peter Cornelius composed Der Barbier von Bagdad as a comic opera in two acts to his own German libretto.\n- Der Barbier von Bagdad was based on \"The Tale of the Tailor\" and \"The Barber’s Stories of his Six Brothers\" from One Thousand and One Nights.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
